{"title":"Cramer's Rule","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Cramer's Rule</b> is a method of solving systems of equations using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Matrix\" title=\"Matrix\">matrices</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"General_Form_for_n_variables\">General Form for n variables</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cramer%27s_Rule&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: General Form for n variables\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Cramer's Rule employs the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinant\">matrix determinant</a> to solve a system of <i>n</i> linear equations in <i>n</i> variables.\n</p><p>We wish to solve the general linear system <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/c/dfce8bdc0f09c855c2ee617d507c38320d78c587.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> for the vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/8/008dbd88b77b137597ef35779a19d4b0d2edc6cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{x} = \\left( \\begin{array}{c} x_1  \\\\  \\vdots \\\\ x_n \\end{array} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -33px\" width=\"97\" height=\"75\" />. Here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is the coefficient matrix, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fccad4b3914168405510f7854bb8f10d4115f56f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{b}$\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is a column vector.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/5/b452578d1e7d735b1a75d7b4ad13b1713406fdd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> be the matrix formed by replacing the jth column of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fccad4b3914168405510f7854bb8f10d4115f56f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{b}$\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Then, Cramer's Rule states that the general solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57eb42437cd31c85efb02ea6ccb2f9fdfb1d4e46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_j = \\frac{|M_j|}{|A|} \\; \\; \\; \\forall j \\in \\mathbb{N}^{\\leq n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"168\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"General_Solution_for_2_Variables\">General Solution for 2 Variables</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cramer%27s_Rule&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: General Solution for 2 Variables\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the following system of linear equations in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, with constants <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/e/29eb2b838e6268513cb6fc621770b6ad4dd97add.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d, r, s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"91\" height=\"16\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/2/6c2ec062b7611bc7fa187b94289abc33a7a14731.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{eqnarray*} ax + cy &amp;=&amp; r\\\\ bx + dy &amp;=&amp; s \\end{eqnarray*}\" width=\"114\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>By Cramer's Rule, the solution to this system is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/5/aa5048b9126a938a3d321578e8663a299ccbccea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}  r &amp; c \\\\ s &amp; d \\end{vmatrix}} {\\begin{vmatrix}  a &amp; c \\\\  b &amp; d \\end{vmatrix}} = \\frac{rd - sc}{ad - bc} \\qquad y = \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}  a &amp; r \\\\ b &amp; s \\end{vmatrix}} {\\begin{vmatrix}  a &amp; c \\\\  b &amp; d \\end{vmatrix}} = \\frac{sa - rb}{ad - cb}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -41px\" width=\"377\" height=\"92\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_in_3_Variables\">Example in 3 Variables</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cramer%27s_Rule&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Example in 3 Variables\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/2/a1242e4a72a101441abd93c49c20a46423adb396.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{eqnarray*} x_1+2x_2+3x_3&amp;=&amp;14\\\\ 3x_1+x_2+2x_3&amp;=&amp;11\\\\ 2x_1+3x_2+x_3&amp;=&amp;11 \\end{eqnarray*}\" width=\"181\" height=\"64\" />\n</p><p>Here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/e/f0e4da9857ad3e80f7af4609f12eebd30b37f819.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\left( \\begin{array}{ccc} 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\ 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\\\\ 2 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 \\end{array} \\right) \\qquad \\mathbf{b} = \\left( \\begin{array}{c} 14\\\\ 11\\\\ 11 \\end{array} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"279\" height=\"64\" />\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/810a34f7ae594de044b314474075a17085914a3e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M_1 = \\left( \\begin{array}{ccc} 14 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\ 11 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\\\\ 11 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 \\end{array} \\right) \\qquad M_2 = \\left( \\begin{array}{ccc} 1 &amp; 14 &amp; 3\\\\ 3 &amp; 11 &amp; 2\\\\ 2 &amp; 11 &amp; 1 \\end{array} \\right) \\qquad M_3 = \\left( \\begin{array}{ccc} 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 14\\\\ 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 11\\\\ 2 &amp; 3 &amp; 11 \\end{array} \\right)\\]\" width=\"566\" height=\"64\" />\n</p><p>We calculate the determinants:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/d/71d2b0cb5acd430ed01cf5b2ccd4df8984eaba62.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|A| = 18 \\qquad |M_1| = 18 \\qquad |M_2| = 36 \\qquad |M_3| = 54\\]\" width=\"396\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Finally, we solve the system:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/3/e23518ccee04b1be493b55b9140594f032a93fe7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_1 = \\frac{|M_1|}{|A|} = \\frac{18}{18}=1 \\qquad x_2 = \\frac{|M_2|}{|A|} = \\frac{36}{18} = 2 \\qquad x_3 = \\frac{|M_3|}{|A|} = \\frac{54}{18} = 3\\]\" width=\"548\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081758\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.013 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.024 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 90/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4332/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5598:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081758 and revision id 199530. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}